@TokisanGames good news, ish. I can reproduce the slowdown. The bad news is that I think it has nothing at all to do with the SceneTreeEditor, and I do suspect that this will not help you.

I will have to have a look at this separately. 

EDIT: yeah, the problem has nothing to do with the number of nodes. One of the node types in your tree (I haven't worked out which one yet) wants to do some kind of housekeeping whenever something changes. I think this might be a problem at runtime as well.

> If there are node or resource types that never ever contain a nodepath they could be skipped entirely. However, because of custom properties that'd would still not work.

Yeah I guess that prevents culling of the Object recursion, but the approach could still be generalized with a new usage flag rather than certain names being treated differently

```c++
if (E.usage & PROPERTY_USAGE_NO_NODEPATH_CHECK) {
	continue;
}

if (!(E.usage & (PROPERTY_USAGE_STORAGE | PROPERTY_USAGE_EDITOR))) {
	continue;
}
```

(mentioning as the current speedup is already post `get_property_list` getting called; maybe I'm misunderstanding why you quoted that part rather than the recursive culling part)
